<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ogasawara Tadazane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1596–1667</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ogasawara Tadazane was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and relative of the Tokugawa who in 1632 received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a> domain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, with 150,000 koku, when the Hosokawa were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a>. Guarding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanmon-straits">Kanmon Straits</a>, he took part in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> of 1637–1638, and his descendants ruled Kokura until the Meiji Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogasawara-tadazane.jpg" alt="Ogasawara Tadazane" width="320" height="707" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ogasawara_Tadazane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
